/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'
  Reporting-Endpoints: csp-endpoint="https://api.cireweddings.com/api/csp-report"
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: https://api.cireweddings.com; connect-src 'self' https://api.cireweddings.com; frame-src 'none'; worker-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; report-uri https://api.cireweddings.com/api/csp-report; report-to csp-endpoint

/claim*
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer

# Long-cache the immutable, content-hashed Astro bundles, the self-hosted font
# files included. Every path under /_astro/ carries a content hash in the
# filename, so a year-long cache is unconditionally safe: a changed file is a
# new URL, never the same one with different bytes.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The same shape as the organiser portal's policy (`cire/host/public/_headers`):
# an ENFORCED line carrying only directives that cannot break a working page,
# plus a REPORT-ONLY line carrying the real lock-down, reporting to cire-api's
# first-party collector until a real-browser pass over the dev tier — sign-in,
# the claim link and the enquiry list — comes back with no reports. Then rename
# the header to `Content-Security-Policy` and drop the three-directive line.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE PRODUCTION POLICY. The build rewrites the copy in `dist/`
# (`src/lib/tier-headers.ts`): every `https://api.cireweddings.com` above
# becomes the origin of the `PUBLIC_CIRE_API_URL` the bundle was built with, so
# the dev tier allows and reports to its own API, and a local
# `wrangler pages dev dist` build allows the local one. Write only the
# production origin here; the build fails if this file stops naming it, or if
# the origin it writes appears in no client script.
#
# NO GOOGLE FONTS in `style-src` or `font-src`: `astro.config.mjs` downloads
# Schibsted Grotesk and Cormorant Garamond at build time via
# `fontProviders.google()`, and none of the three page shells links
# fonts.googleapis.com. It matters most on `/claim`, which is opened straight
# from an emailed invite: a Google stylesheet there would tell Google about
# every vendor who followed the link, before rendering a word.
#
# `script-src 'unsafe-inline'` is load-bearing: the theme-boot script
# (`THEME_BOOT_SCRIPT`) is `is:inline` on all three shells so it can run before
# first paint, and an inline script cannot be an external hashed file. Astro's
# island bootstrap is external `/_astro/*` and covered by 'self'.
#
# Other differences from `cire/host/public/_headers`, audited from source:
#   - No `blob:` in `img-src`: no cropper, no object URLs (`data:` is kept for
#     inline SVG data URIs).
#   - `connect-src` is cire-api only. Sign-in is a top-level redirect through
#     cire/api's OIDC leg and account management links out to musubi — both
#     navigations, which no `connect-src` governs.
#
# On avatars: `AccountAvatar` renders the OIDC `picture` claim, which can point
# at ANY https host — it is unvalidated at every hop between osn-api and here,
# so the sink (`httpsAvatarUrl`) is what enforces the scheme. `img-src` allows
# no avatar host, and while this policy is report-only it does not restrain the
# request either: a set avatar would load and file a violation report. OSN hosts
# no avatars yet (osn-api writes `avatarUrl` null on registration and on
# erasure, and has no update path), so nothing populates the claim. Once
# enforced, an avatar from a host not listed here is blocked and
# `AccountAvatar` shows the initial instead. Add OSN's avatar host here when it
# exists: englishstventures/osn#1207.
#
# The `/claim*` rule above only overrides `Referrer-Policy` (a claim token rides
# in the URL); Cloudflare merges the rules, so that path keeps both CSP headers.
